(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-blocks-ventricular-arrhythmias-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Arrhythmias II: Blocks and Ventriculars Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which causes should be checked first in a patient with frequent premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["Hypertension, diabetes","Dehydration, fever","Hypoxemia, electrolytes","Hyperlipidemia, obesity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxemia and electrolyte imbalances (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypercalcemia) are the main causes, so new frequent premature ventricular contractions call for checking oxygen and electrolytes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Sudden cardiac death is defined as unexpected natural death from cardiac causes within what time of collapse?","choices":["Ten minutes","One hour","Six hours","Thirty days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sudden cardiac death is generally defined as unexpected natural death from cardiac causes occurring within 1 hour of the patient's collapse. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"After transcutaneous pacing stabilizes a patient in third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block, which is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Wearable defibrillator","Permanent pacemaker","Oral beta blocker","Catheter ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Emergent transcutaneous pacing bridges the patient, and the definitive treatment for complete heart block is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Once an advanced airway is in place during cardiac arrest, how are breaths given with continuous compressions?","choices":["One breath every 2 seconds","Compressions without breaths","One breath every 6 seconds","Two breaths per 30 compressions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once intubated, cardiopulmonary resuscitation becomes continuous chest compressions with 1 breath every 6 seconds. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which electrolyte is given as an intravenous push and then as an infusion to break torsades de pointes?","choices":["Magnesium","Potassium","Calcium","Phosphate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades de pointes, and more magnesium is then given as an infusion; it is the main treatment. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which degenerative, fibrotic disease of the conduction system is a named cause of left bundle branch block?","choices":["Lyme carditis","Chagas disease","Takotsubo syndrome","Lenègre-Lev disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Degenerative or fibrotic disease of the conduction system, Lenègre-Lev disease, is listed among the causes of left bundle branch block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which finding defines hemodynamic instability in ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Altered mental status","Heart rate of 130","Mild palpitations","Wide QRS complexes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Instability is defined as any one of chest pain, altered mental status, shortness of breath or hypotension. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which treatment is used for symptomatic left bundle branch block with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Catheter ablation","Biventricular pacemaker","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Left bundle branch block is usually left untreated, except symptomatic block with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, which is treated with a biventricular pacer. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic can be used in torsades de pointes?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous metoprolol","Intravenous lidocaine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intravenous lidocaine is the antiarrhythmic that can be used in torsades, though the evidence for it is limited; magnesium remains the main treatment. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which is the management of asymptomatic Mobitz type I block?","choices":["No treatment needed","Permanent pacemaker now","Transcutaneous pacing","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type I needs no treatment unless there are debilitating symptoms or heart failure symptoms; a permanent pacemaker is considered only for symptomatic patients. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram finding defines first-degree atrioventricular block?","choices":["P waves unrelated to the QRS","Constant PR interval over 0.20 seconds","PR lengthening until a QRS drops","Constant PR with random dropped QRS"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-degree atrioventricular block is a prolonged PR interval, greater than 0.20 seconds and constant, with one P wave for every narrow QRS complex. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does Mobitz type I block usually present?","choices":["With palpitations in the neck","With heart failure symptoms","Without any symptoms","With fainting spells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type I block is usually asymptomatic. When symptoms do occur they are dizziness, fatigue, exertional intolerance and syncope, and a symptomatic patient is considered for a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which medication is a named cause of first-degree atrioventricular block?","choices":["Digoxin","Furosemide","Amlodipine","Lisinopril"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin all slow atrioventricular conduction and are named causes of first-degree block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which mechanism makes atrioventricular block more common in elderly patients?","choices":["Shrinking atrial size","Degenerative conduction disease","Rising vagal tone with age","Age-related Lyme exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Age-related degenerative change of the conduction system is the most common cause of first-degree block and drives the His-Purkinje disease behind Mobitz type II and complete block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the treatment of right bundle branch block depend on?","choices":["The rsR' amplitude","The underlying condition","The QRS width alone","The patient's heart rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Right bundle branch block is usually asymptomatic, and its treatment depends on the underlying condition; prognosis depends on the presence and type of heart disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"For a low-risk patient with symptomatic premature ventricular contractions, which treatment is first-line?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Implantable defibrillator","Beta blocker","Amiodarone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In low-risk patients with symptoms, a beta blocker is first-line; catheter ablation is kept for symptoms refractory to it or a patient who cannot tolerate drug therapy. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"After defibrillation for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, which procedure is urgent?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Pericardiocentesis","Exercise stress test","Coronary angiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is defibrillated, followed by urgent coronary angiography with possible revascularization, because acute ischemia is its most common cause. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Because Mobitz type II block usually originates at the bundle of His, what is true of its escape rhythm?","choices":["Sinus driven","Above 60 per minute","Weak and unreliable","Fast and narrow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because the block sits low, at the bundle of His, the escape rhythm is weak and unreliable, which adds to the risk of sudden death. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which arrest rhythms carry the best prognosis?","choices":["Fibrillation, asystole","Tachycardia, pulseless electrical","Ventricular tachycardia, fibrillation","Asystole, pulseless electrical"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Among cardiac arrest rhythms, ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation have the best prognosis; asystole and pulseless electrical activity are poor. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which electrical treatment is delivered for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Transcutaneous pacing","Drugs first, shock later","Unsynchronized defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable and its QRS shape keeps changing, so it is defibrillated (unsynchronized) even when a pulse is present. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Into which two fascicles does the left bundle branch divide?","choices":["Anterior and posterior fascicles","Right and left fascicles","Inner and outer fascicles","Medial and apical fascicles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Past the bundle of His the conduction system splits into right and left bundle branches, and the left bundle divides into anterior and posterior fascicles, where a fascicular block can occur. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which role does non-sustained ventricular tachycardia alone play in the decision to place an implantable defibrillator?","choices":["Reason enough for one","A contraindication to one","Needs a wearable vest first","Not an indication by itself"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-sustained ventricular tachycardia in and of itself is not an indication for a defibrillator, although some patients qualify for one for primary prevention of sudden death. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which complication of bacterial endocarditis is a named cause of third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block?","choices":["Splinter hemorrhages","Septic lung emboli","Glomerulonephritis","An associated abscess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bacterial endocarditis with an associated abscess is listed among the causes of complete block, because the infection can extend into the conduction system. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"When is catheter ablation used for low-risk patients with symptomatic premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["When a beta blocker fails","As the very first step","When the burden is under 1%","Whenever bigeminy appears"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A beta blocker is first-line; catheter ablation is used when symptoms are refractory or the patient cannot tolerate drug therapy. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"When left bundle branch block does cause symptoms, what do they resemble?","choices":["Asthma attack","Panic attack","Hypoglycemia","Heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptomatic left bundle branch block resembles a congestive heart failure exacerbation, with dizziness, shortness of breath and dyspnea on exertion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"For premature ventricular contractions under 1% of beats with no structural heart disease and no symptoms, which is the plan?","choices":["Lifelong beta blocker","Catheter ablation","Implantable defibrillator","Reassure and remove triggers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low-risk patients with a low burden (under 1% or 1,000 a day), no structural heart disease and no symptoms need reassurance and elimination of triggers. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"For hemodynamically stable, sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, which approach is used?","choices":["Intravenous adenosine","Intravenous antiarrhythmic","Immediate defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stable, monomorphic sustained ventricular tachycardia is pharmacologically cardioverted with intravenous lidocaine, amiodarone or procainamide, after a monitor, intravenous access, a 12-lead electrocardiogram, oxygen and labs. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which infiltrative diseases are named causes of third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block?","choices":["Asthma and emphysema","Amyloidosis and sarcoidosis","Cirrhosis and pancreatitis","Osteoporosis and gout"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inflammatory and infiltrative disease, namely myocarditis, amyloidosis and sarcoidosis, is listed among the causes of complete heart block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which finding in lead V1 is expected in left bundle branch block?","choices":["Dominant S wave","rsR' pattern","Delta wave","Tall, narrow R"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Left bundle branch block shows a QRS over 0.12 seconds with a dominant S wave in V1 (an rS pattern), a monophasic R in the lateral leads and no Q waves in I, V5 and V6. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"In a high-risk patient with premature ventricular contractions but no isolated beat-induced cardiomyopathy, what is the first step?","choices":["Beta blocker, optimize disease","Reassurance and follow-up","Immediate defibrillation","Intravenous magnesium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Outside isolated premature ventricular contraction-induced cardiomyopathy, the first step in high-risk patients is a beta blocker and optimized treatment of the underlying cardiac disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"}]};
